Abandoning "sell at 3% and repeat" was unambiguously correct. That metric correlated −0.080 with forward return and scored your four best trades at 5.6, 10.0 and 17.4 out of 100 — it rejected DYCL outright. You were using a rule that actively deselected your winners.

What drifted. The goal has been redefined about four times: 3% scalp → 30-session move profile → momentum ranker → dip-buying swing. That's not refinement converging on something; that's oscillation. And today alone the shortlist went 10 → 3 → deleted, and the price band went 1400 → 5400 → 1400 → 1600. That's thrash, not progress.

The sharpest thing I can tell you: you froze this model on the 20th — "this model is closed off now, we will revisit once we have proper data for at least 1 week." That freeze lasted two days. Since then the lookback went 180→60, a shortlist was added and removed, the file was split in two, and an entirely new strategy was built. None of it had the week of data you asked for.

And the mechanism that broke the freeze was backtests. Each change had numbers behind it, so each felt justified. But today I discovered my own N sweep was measuring date-groups of 5 candidates when a live run has 75 — the conclusion I gave you was wrong by a factor of two, and I'd already written it into the config as settled. That's the third finding I've retracted in this session. Backtests kept licensing changes, and the backtests were not reliable enough to license them.

So where you've landed is more complex than where you started, with two scanners, one of them a day old and explicitly possibly-overfit, and zero live validation of either. That's not obviously better than one frozen model you'd followed for a month.

What I'd focus on:

Freeze, properly this time. No config changes, no new strategies, until signal_outcomes has real graded data. That's December. If I propose a change before then with a backtest behind it, the right answer is no — that's exactly how the last freeze broke.

Do the one thing that doesn't depend on any backtest: size every position the same. Equal-weighting your existing 19 positions turns −₹10,412 into +₹6,506. Same stocks, same entries, ₹16,918 difference. That's larger than any edge either scanner has demonstrated, it requires no validation, and it fixes the actual hole in your P&L — BSE at 30.8% of capital.

Follow momentum, watch swings. Momentum has 163 decision dates behind it. The swing scanner has ~5 independent windows. Treat its list as a watchlist you're auditing, not instructions.

The four months weren't wasted — you have working infrastructure, honest data, and a record of what's been tested. What's missing isn't more analysis. It's a period of not changing anything.
